 that tells you all about the functions  of the capture card and how to set it up  and right out of the box you can see  that this is the elgato 4k60s plus  you can see some very nice patterns over  here as well as the elgato logo  obviously and facing the back you can  see that we have a few  ports  over here starting with the hdmi in and  out ports as well as two usbc ports one  is for power and one is to link up to  your pc and moving to the front of the  panel you can see that we have the sd  card slot as well as the touch  capacitive record button  as well as the 3.5 mm line in out of the  box you also got your main power supply  along with a slew of international  adapters  let's say all the adapters are simple  snap on and snap off function  like shell  which makes this whole package great for  portability and lastly in the box you  get three cables one being the hdmi 2.0  cables and your two usbc cables right  now the best part about this that all  the cables have a nice premium feel to  them and they're even gold plated so you  know you're getting the good stuff here  guys and that's all there is to it  honestly it's really meant to be a  standalone game capture card device so  once you set all of this up you're good  to go now it is recommended that you use  an sdxc card that being said agato  recommends an sd card that has at least  a uhs class 3 or a v30 now it is also  important that you get an sd card that  has a higher storage capacity we're  talking about at least 128 gigabytes to  256 gigabytes you could also use a  microsd card with an adapter that has  the recommended class and storage  capacity of course you could also use up  to one terabyte of storage capacity but  over one terabytes have not been tested  by agato and it may not work so you  should be able to get six to seven hours  of gameplay footage at 4k 60fps on a  single  gigabyte sd card and you can either  format it to fat32 or exfat keep in mind  that fat32 is going to split your files  into every four gigabytes or if you're  like me and prefer to just have one  chunky file then exfat is the way to go  so now the beauty of the elgato 4k 60s  plus is that it's a standalone game  capture device  this means that you don't have to plug  into a pc so all you need to do is plug  it in pop an sd card hit record on the  touch sensor  and you're good to go it's easy as that  and if you're a streamer and you want to  use a pc you can do that too you can  wire your 4k 60s plus with the  additional usb c to usb a cable you can  record it standalone on your pc and  capture gameplay footage direct to the  sd card alternatively you can use the  4k60s plus with third-party broadcasting  software like obs and xsplit so you have  some flexibility there  now one good thing about the 4k60s plus  is that it not only records in 4k 60fps  but in hdr as well  so if you're playing hdr you have the  ability to do that  it basically records whatever you put  into it so if you're playing hdr it  records in hdr you also have the option  to record an sdr so you can still use  this to record 1080 or even 2k on pc it  is fully supported now we've been using  the s plus as a standalone with the ps5  as both a way of recording gameplay  footage to an sd card and as a pass  through device to play games on the tv  and doing either of those things we  absolutely had no hitch and in fact it  actually works better than we expected  it to now it's worth noting that this  has a leg free pass through feature if  you've just connected it to a tv or a  bigger screen but other than that well  it's not really lag free while live  streaming  so you will need to offset that in your  live stream software so that you can be  in sync with your webcam and your audio  now another neat feature about this  device is that you're able to control  the bitrate and recording settings such  as recording it at raw 4k  now it really goes a little more into  the technical side of things where you  actually have to edit a settings.txt  file in the sd card itself  which agato automatically places on the  sd card so you're probably asking  yourself what happens when you're  recording and reach the end of the card  capacity so the 4k 60s plus will leave  about 90 megabytes or so free so it can  properly finish off the file afterwards  the recording simply stops but it will  not be corrupted so heads off to the  folks at elgato for really creating this  very neat feature the file size is  manageable and editing suites like  premiere pro works very smoothly on it  not to mention that all the gameplay  footage you're seeing in the background  are all shot in 4k 60fps  so it's really a fantastic product  coming from gato of course this comes at  a very hefty price tag of 400 us dollars  which might sway some buyers but this is  definitely a godsend when it comes to  live streamers and content creators who  want to bring their capture capabilities  on the road without having to lug around  pcs or figure out wires and splitters  now the 4k 60s plus is definitely the  best and the best in creating gaming  content and perhaps more its standalone  performance is perhaps what's considered  well at least for us it's the most ideal  option that gives you the tools and  power you need for its convenience size  and weight and two is standalone  recording without needing a pc  makes this probably one of the best  capture cards money can buy so yeah  that's it for this video we hope you  enjoyed this one and if you found it  helpful do give us a thumbs up what do  you think about the 4k60s plus and  perhaps other gacho products let us know  in the comments below and if you haven't  already do consider subscribing and  smashing the bell icon so you can be  notified every time we upload a new  video  yeah  until then this is zaki signing off  adios muchachos  [Music]  [Music]
 

